 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: `GetVoxel` is O(1) for Uniform, O(1) + decompression time for Compressed, O(1) for Resident. `SetVoxel` is O(1) amortized, O(N) if detachment or re-allocation is needed. `TryCompress` is O(N) for uniformity check, O(N) for compression. `ComputeUniformValue` is O(N) with word-aligned optimizations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: Resident is O(N) where N = ChunkSize^3. Uniform is O(1) (stores one value). Compressed is O(C) where C is compressed size, typically < N. Empty is O(1) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance**: For a 32^3 chunk, raw data is ~128 KB. Compression can significantly reduce this for uniform or repetitive data. Async compression is keyed on distance, allowing far chunks to be compressed without blocking the main thread [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scaling**: Scales well with chunk size; larger chunks benefit more from compression if data is uniform or compressible. Distance-based streaming ensures only relevant chunks are kept in high-residency states [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Reduces memory footprint for large worlds. Asynchronous compression minimizes frame-time spikes. Versioning ensures data consistency across concurrent accesses [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Compression overhead for small or highly varied chunks. Complexity in managing residency states and versioning. Requires careful handling of negative coordinates and boundary conditions [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use**: Infinite voxel worlds with distance-based streaming. Scenes with large uniform areas (e.g., sky, deep ocean). When memory is a constraint and compression ratios are favorable [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use**: Small, static worlds where memory is not a concern. Highly dynamic, non-uniform data where compression overhead outweighs benefits [S1, unofficial].
